2026-06-15 12:47:47,299 articletxt/485.txt
2026-06-15 12:47:47,299 TITLE: Mechanical pretreatments of different agri-based feedstock in full-scale biogas plants under real operational conditions Title of processing article
2026-06-15 12:48:16,270 {
  "pretreatments": [
    {
      "id": 0,
      "description": "Knife Milling (KM): Applied to substrates with total solids content higher than 85%. Operates inside a milling chamber containing mobile milling elements mounted on a central shaft. It applies mechanical forces to disaggregate solid particles and slice fibers through cutting fragments and dissociating lignocellulosic biomass via impact and shear between the knives and an internal sieve. This process produces small chopped pieces, reduces coarse particles (>5 mm) by 99%, and increases specific surface area by up to 189%."
    },
    {
      "id": 1,
      "description": "Hammer Milling (HM): Applied to substrates with total solids content ranging between 20% and 85%. Consists of a rotor equipped with a set of attached hammers that turn and impact the feed material against a breaker plate. Mechanical stress is driven by an impact zone and compression forces resulting from collisions between the hammers and the breaker plate, which remove fragments and dissociate pieces into long thin fibers. This treatment reduces particles >5 mm by 33% and increases specific surface area by 17%."
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "description": "Extrusion (EX): Raw materials are fed into one end of a system where a driving screw transports them along a barrel. As the material moves, it is subjected to friction heat, mixing, and vigorous shearing forces upon pressure release at the finishing end. The primary mechanical stresses arise from shear and friction between the rotating screw and the extruder walls, causing considerable abrasion, breakdown of cell walls, and structural deformation. This treatment reduces particles >5 mm by 40% and increases specific surface area by 63%."
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "description": "Wet Shredding: Implemented as a wet shredder integrated with a heavy material separator within the pretreatment line. It homogenizes fluid substrates and performs initial size reduction while protecting downstream equipment from foreign matter damage, blockages, and pump clogging. Acts on solid and liquid substrate mixtures before subsequent cavitation treatment, modifying the physical structure and rheology of the feedstock."
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "description": "Hydrodynamic Cavitation: Utilizes a stator and rotor assembly where a solid cylinder rotor (connected to a variable frequency drive) controls rotation speed, energy input, and cavitation intensity. The process generates cavitation through the formation, growth, and implosive collapse of microbubbles in the liquid carrier. This implosion creates extreme localized temperature and pressure, along with massive shear forces that disintegrate lignocellulosic structures, making fibers more accessible to microbial degradation. Applied in recirculation on digestate, it reduces particles >5 mm by 50% and increases specific surface area by 9%."
    }
  ]
}
